- id: 11
  question: "Which field in a Pod spec controls how many times a container is restarted after it exits with a non‑zero status?"
  options:
    A: "restartPolicy"
    B: "terminationGracePeriodSeconds"
    C: "backoffLimit"
    D: "livenessProbe"
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "restartPolicy defines the Pod restart behavior (Always, OnFailure, Never). (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-lifecycle/#restart-policy)"
    B: "terminationGracePeriodSeconds sets the time to wait before force‑killing a Pod. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/container-lifecycle-hooks/#termination)"
    C: "backoffLimit applies to Jobs, not plain Pods. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/job/#backoff-limit)"
    D: "livenessProbe determines health checks, not restart count. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-lifecycle/#container-probes)"

- id: 12
  question: "What annotation can be added to a Service to enable the AWS Load Balancer Controller to provision an NLB?"
  options:
    A: "service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: nlb"
    B: "service.kubernetes.io/aws-nlb: true"
    C: "service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-internal: 0.0.0.0/0"
    D: "service.kubernetes.io/load-balancer: nlb"
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "The annotation service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: nlb tells the controller to create a Network Load Balancer. (https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/aws-load-balancer-controller/v2.6/guide/service/annotations/)"
    B: "This annotation does not exist in the controller spec."
    C: "This annotation configures internal ELB, not NLB type."
    D: "Incorrect annotation key; the controller uses the service.beta.kubernetes.io prefix."

- id: 13
  question: "In a Deployment manifest, which field determines the maximum number of Pods that can be unavailable during a rolling update?"
  options:
    A: "maxSurge"
    B: "maxUnavailable"
    C: "revisionHistoryLimit"
    D: "progressDeadlineSeconds"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "maxSurge controls the number of extra Pods created during update, not unavailable ones. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/#strategy)"
    B: "maxUnavailable sets how many Pods can be down during the rollout. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/#strategy)"
    C: "revisionHistoryLimit defines how many old ReplicaSets to retain."
    D: "progressDeadlineSeconds is the timeout for a rollout to be considered stalled."

- id: 14
  question: "Which Kubernetes object is used to grant a set of permissions to a group of users or service accounts?"
  options:
    A: "Role"
    B: "ClusterRole"
    C: "RoleBinding"
    D: "NetworkPolicy"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "Role is namespace‑scoped; it does not grant cluster‑wide permissions. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/#role-and-clusterrole)"
    B: "ClusterRole can be used across the whole cluster and can be bound via ClusterRoleBinding. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/#role-and-clusterrole)"
    C: "RoleBinding ties a Role to subjects; it does not define permissions itself."
    D: "NetworkPolicy controls network traffic, not RBAC permissions."

- id: 15
  question: "What is the purpose of the 'imagePullSecrets' field in a Pod spec?"
  options:
    A: "Specify a list of Docker image tags to pull"
    B: "Provide credentials for pulling images from a private registry"
    C: "Define the order in which images are pulled"
    D: "Encrypt the image layers before runtime"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "imagePullSecrets does not list tags; it references Secret objects."
    B: "imagePullSecrets references Secret objects that contain registry credentials. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/pull-image-private-registry/)"
    C: "Pull order is not controlled by this field."
    D: "Encryption of image layers is handled by the container runtime, not by this field."

- id: 16
  question: "Which of the following is NOT a valid value for the 'restartPolicy' field in a Pod spec?"
  options:
    A: "Always"
    B: "OnFailure"
    C: "Never"
    D: "OnExit"
  answer: D
  explanations:
    A: "Always is a standard restart policy. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-lifecycle/#restart-policy)"
    B: "OnFailure restarts the container only when it exits with non‑zero code."
    C: "Never disables automatic restarts."
    D: "OnExit is not a supported restart policy."

- id: 17
  question: "When defining a Service of type 'ExternalName', which field must be provided?"
  options:
    A: "externalIPs"
    B: "loadBalancerIP"
    C: "externalName"
    D: "clusterIP"
  answer: C
  explanations:
    A: "externalIPs is for external IP addresses, not for ExternalName services."
    B: "loadBalancerIP applies to LoadBalancer services."
    C: "externalName holds the DNS name to which the Service will resolve. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#externalname)"
    D: "clusterIP is allocated automatically; not required for ExternalName."

- id: 18
  question: "Which command shows the events that have occurred on a specific node?"
  options:
    A: "kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.kind=Node"
    B: "kubectl describe node <node-name>"
    C: "kubectl logs node <node-name>"
    D: "kubectl get pods --all-namespaces --field-selector spec.nodeName=<node-name>"
  answer: B
  explanations:
    A: "Field selector can filter events, but describing the node directly shows its recent events more clearly."
    B: "kubectl describe node prints the Events section for that node. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/overview/#kubectl-describe)"
    C: "kubectl logs works on Pods, not nodes."
    D: "This lists Pods scheduled on the node, not node events."

- id: 19
  question: "In a HorizontalPodAutoscaler (HPA) spec, which metric type can be used to scale based on custom application metrics?"
  options:
    A: "Resource"
    B: "Object"
    C: "External"
    D: "Pods"
  answer: C
  explanations:
    A: "Resource metrics are built‑in like CPU/memory."
    B: "Object metrics refer to metrics of another Kubernetes object."
    C: "External metrics allow scaling based on metrics outside the cluster, often custom app metrics. (https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale/#support-for-custom-metrics)"
    D: "Pods is not a valid metric type for HPA."

- id: 20
  question: "What does the 'kubectl top pod' command require to be installed in the cluster?"
  options:
    A: "Metrics Server"
    B: "Heapster"
    C: "Prometheus Adapter"
    D: "Kube‑state‑metrics"
  answer: A
  explanations:
    A: "Metrics Server provides resource usage metrics for the top command. (https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server)"
    B: "Heapster is deprecated in favor of Metrics Server."
    C: "Prometheus Adapter is for custom metrics, not the basic top command."
    D: "kube‑state‑metrics exposes object state, not resource usage."
